# Route from the code

This is the default. Read the tree, manifests, and the files in the change before you name a skill. Open only the matches, one `SKILL.md` at a time.

| What you read | Open |
| --- | --- |
| Secrets, auth, or permissions | senior security |
| Personal data in logs or storage | privacy |
| Screens, CLI output, or error states | product design |
| User-facing copy | content design |
| Tests or untested branches | QA |
| CI, Docker, or deploy scripts | DevOps |
| Timeouts, health, or rollback | SRE |
| SQL or migrations | database and SQL |
| HTTP or RPC contracts | API design |
| A language manifest or source file | that language's skill |
| A commit, branch, pull request, conflict, or git history | the matching git skill |
| Docker, a lockfile, config, logs, a flag, a job, a cache, or a monorepo | the matching workflow skill |

Say which skill you will open and the file that triggered it. Do not open the rest.

List proposed rules as add or modify, then ask. Wait. Write only what the user accepts, under `.cursor/rules/` in the project being reviewed.

```text
BAD:  Open every role, then write the rule files.
GOOD: Open the skill that matches the file you read, and ask before adding or modifying a rule.
```
